invariant: INV-26
file: quic_aes_hw.c
catches: aes_equiv_test
reason: the AES=hw key expansion starts from the wrong round constant, so
  it computes a different key schedule from AES=soft and a different
  block from it. CBMC cannot read an intrinsic, so no proof covers this
  file and bin/aes_equiv_test is the whole of what holds it: it runs both
  implementations over the same keys and blocks and compares the round
  keys and the cipher output byte for byte. The edit lands in
  aes_expand_round_keys, which sits outside the two architecture arms, so
  it is compiled on an ARMv8 host and an x86-64 one alike and the catch
  does not depend on which instruction set the runner has. Without the
  equivalence test this divergence reaches a build that reads every QUIC
  Initial packet wrong and no gate says so.

--- old
    uint8_t round_constant = 0x01;
--- new
    uint8_t round_constant = 0x02;
